1. Evaluate the effectiveness of your concept map in representing cloud computing principles. Which design decisions most strongly support your solution, and why?
The concept map effectively represents cloud computing by connecting important concepts such as service models, APIs, virtualization, scalability, elasticity, 
resource pooling, and cloud services. The use of labeled relationships makes it easy to understand how each component supports the overall cloud architecture.
The strongest design decisions are the selection of suitable service models, use of APIs and web services, and separation of provider and user responsibilities.
These decisions clearly show how cloud resources are provided and consumed. The concept map also shows how virtualization enables efficient resource sharing and 
how scalability and elasticity help the system handle changing workloads.

2. Analyze how scalability, elasticity, and resource management influence the architecture shown in your concept map. What challenges could arise if these capabilities 
were absent?
Scalability allows the system to increase or decrease its computing resources according to the workload. Elasticity enables resources to be automatically adjusted when 
demand changes. Resource management ensures that computing, storage, and network resources are used efficiently.These capabilities improve system performance and 
prevent resource wastage. For example, during high user traffic, additional resources can be provided to maintain performance. If scalability and elasticity were absent, 
the system could become slow or unavailable during peak usage. It could also lead to higher costs, resource wastage, poor performance, and service interruptions.

3. Justify the selection of the APIs, web services, or cloud services included in your concept map. How do they contribute to the overall functionality and efficiency 
of the system?
APIs, REST services, and web services are included because they allow different components of the cloud system to communicate with each other. REST APIs provide a lightweight
and efficient method for communication between the frontend, backend, databases, and other cloud services.Cloud services such as compute, database, storage, 
authentication, monitoring, and analytics support different application requirements without requiring all infrastructure to be managed manually. APIs also improve 
integration, flexibility, and reusability. Therefore, these services make the system more efficient, scalable, and easier to maintain.

4. Critically examine the relationships between the components in your concept map. How do these interactions improve reliability, performance, or user experience?
The components in the concept map are connected through clear relationships such as provides, uses, enables, communicates through, and supports. The user application 
communicates with the backend through APIs, while the backend interacts with databases, storage, authentication, and other cloud services.These interactions improve 
reliability by separating different responsibilities and allowing services to be monitored independently. They improve performance through scalable computing and 
efficient resource management. Authentication and security services protect user data, while monitoring helps identify problems quickly. Together, these components 
provide a faster, more reliable, secure, and user-friendly system.

5. Reflect on the process of developing your concept map. How has creating and analyzing the map changed your understanding of cloud computing architecture, and
what improvements would you make if you were redesigning it?
Developing the concept map helped me understand that cloud computing is not limited to storing data online. It involves the interaction of service models, virtualization, 
APIs, cloud resources, scalability, elasticity, security, and resource management.Creating the relationships between these components made it easier to understand 
how a cloud-based system works as a complete architecture. If I redesigned the map, I would improve the visual hierarchy, reduce unnecessary connections, and 
clearly separate service models, cloud infrastructure, APIs, and security components. I would also add more specific project-related examples to make the architecture 
easier to understand.
